THE LEADER GRIPPER GHOST: a 50 %-transparent SO-101 *leader* gripper locked to
the operator's controller, drawn on top of the follower arm it is driving.

INCLUDED AFTER `<include file="so101.xml"/>`, AND THAT ORDER IS THE WHOLE
TRANSPARENCY STORY. `mjv_updateScene` emits geoms in GEOM-ID ORDER -- measured,
not assumed, and `tests/test_ghost.py` asserts it on this very scene. A body
declared after the robot therefore gets higher geom ids, lands last in
`mjvScene`, and is drawn last by `cpp/scene_renderer.cpp`'s single pass. With
`depthWriteEnable = TRUE` and `srcAlpha / oneMinusSrcAlpha` that blends
correctly *because nothing is drawn after it*. Alpha works out too:
ghost(0.5) over robot(1.0) gives A = 0.5 + 0.5*1 = 1.0, so there is no
passthrough hole where the ghost overlaps the arm.

NO RENDERER CHANGE WAS MADE, and a second Vulkan pipeline with
`depthWriteEnable = FALSE` was considered and rejected as premature. TWO
RESIDUAL RISKS ARE DOCUMENTED RATHER THAN FIXED, and the remedy for both is
that second pipeline -- build it if either bites on a headset, not before:
  1. the ghost writes DEPTH into the buffer CloudXR reprojects against, so a
     translucent surface can pull reprojected geometry toward the operator;
  2. ghost-vs-ghost self-overlap. `cullMode` is VK_CULL_MODE_NONE during
     bring-up, so back faces of the ghost blend against its own front faces and
     the overlapping regions read darker than the rest.

PROVENANCE. The three meshes are TheRobotStudio/SO-ARM100
`STL/SO101/Individual/{Wrist_Roll,Trigger,Handle}_SO101.stl`, at commit
fda892cba81032c46c40976a48c9ceadbf40a9ca, Apache-2.0 (LICENSE is vendored
beside them). They are VENDORED rather than fetched: 1.3 MB total, and it keeps
the ghost testable without a second fetch step.

THE STLs ARE NOT IN PRINT ORIENTATION. That was asserted early in this project
and it is wrong -- they sit on a shared CAD datum:

    Handle_SO101.stl        z[ 36.703,  96.768]   <- floats 37 mm above z=0
    Trigger_SO101.stl       z[-26.000,   6.000]   <- straddles zero
    Wrist_Roll_SO101.stl    x[-35.200,  30.000] y[-24.000, 28.000]
    wrist_roll_follower...  x[-35.000,  30.000] y[-24.000, 28.000]  <- SAME DATUM

Nothing on a print bed floats 37 mm above the plate; the print PLATES are the
separate `STL/SO101/Leader/{Prusa,Ender}_*.stl` files. `Wrist_Roll_SO101` is the
follower's shank with the fixed jaw amputated, and its bounds are Menagerie's
mesh x 1000.

ALL THREE TRANSFORMS BELOW ARE THEREFORE DERIVED, not hand-tuned -- including
the handle, which the brief this file was written to expected to be seven
unknown numbers. See the geom comments; the assembled gaps are the evidence.

`scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"`: SO-ARM STLs are in MILLIMETRES and Menagerie is in
metres. Getting this wrong does NOT read as "a big mesh" -- the camera ends up
inside a 65 m solid with culling off, and the symptom is a full-screen
50 %-alpha wash that reads as "passthrough broke".
-->
<mujoco model="so101 leader gripper ghost">
  <asset>
    <!-- Paths are relative to THIS file's directory. MuJoCo resolves an
         included file's assets against the include-relative directory, which is
         why these three names carry no prefix and why this fragment lives in
         the same directory as the STLs it names. -->
    <mesh name="leader_wrist_roll" file="Wrist_Roll_SO101.stl"
          scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="leader_trigger" file="Trigger_SO101.stl"
          scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="leader_handle" file="Handle_SO101.stl"
          scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <!-- alpha 0.5. The ghost is meant to be read THROUGH: the follower's own
         jaw has to stay legible underneath it, because the gap between the two
         is the whole point of showing it. -->
    <material name="leader_ghost" rgba="0.55 0.80 1.00 0.5"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- A MOCAP BODY, not decor geoms appended to mjvScene by hand. MuJoCo then
         owns the mesh_pos/mesh_quat composition, which it applies at compile
         time and which is REQUIRED and silently wrong if skipped: measured on
         the moving jaw, raw STL z spans [-5e-5, 0.1054] while MuJoCo's own mesh
         frame gives z[-0.035, 0.0718] -- a COM translation plus a
         principal-axis rotation. Hand-placed decor geoms would have to
         reproduce both, from mjModel fields most people do not know exist.

         `pos` here is where the ghost sits until the first VALID controller
         sample, and in --mode window / offscreen that is forever -- which is
         what makes the whole overlay pipeline verifiable with no headset.
         Chosen to be in frame of the debug camera and clear of the arm. -->
    <body name="leader_ghost" mocap="true" pos="0.12 0.16 0.22"
          quat="0.5 0.5 -0.5 -0.5">
      <!-- DERIVED, NOT TUNED. so101.xml puts the follower's fixed-jaw shank at
           pos="0 -0.000218 0.00095" quat="0 1 0 0" in the `gripper` body, and
           Wrist_Roll_SO101.stl is that same part on the same datum with the
           fixed jaw amputated. So this slot is Menagerie's, verbatim. -->
      <geom name="leader_ghost_wrist_roll" type="mesh" mesh="leader_wrist_roll"
            material="leader_ghost" pos="0 -0.000218 0.00095" quat="0 1 0 0"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" group="2"/>

      <!-- DERIVED. The trigger occupies the follower's `moving_jaw_so101_v1`
           slot: body pos="0.0202 0.0188 -0.0234" quat="1 1 0 0" composed with
           that body's own visual geom pos="0 0 0.0189", which gives
           pos="0.0202 -0.0001 -0.0234" in the gripper frame.

           DRAWN AT THE OPEN POSE AND IT DOES NOT ARTICULATE. The trigger is a
           rigid child here: a hinged ghost jaw would need a second mocap
           channel and would show the operator their own trigger, which they can
           already feel. What they cannot see is where the FOLLOWER's jaw is,
           and that is drawn underneath. -->
      <geom name="leader_ghost_trigger" type="mesh" mesh="leader_trigger"
            material="leader_ghost" pos="0.0202 -0.0001 -0.0234" quat="1 1 0 0"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" group="2"/>

      <!-- DERIVED TOO, and this CORRECTS the brief this file was written to,
           which said the handle was seven unknown hand-tuned numbers. It is not
           unknown: it is on the same CAD datum as the shank, so it takes the
           SAME transform, and the assembly proves itself.

           Raw STL bounds, in the STLs' own millimetres:

               Wrist_Roll_SO101      x[-35.200, 30.000] y[-24.000, 28.000] z[ -1.000, 41.850]
               wrist_roll_follower   x[-35.000, 30.000] y[-24.000, 28.000] z[  0.000,105.000]
               Handle_SO101          x[-93.879,-5.754]  y[-12.500, 12.500] z[ 36.703, 96.768]

           The first two agree on x, on y and on the z origin: the leader part IS
           the follower's shank, amputated at z = 41.85 instead of running out to
           105. The handle then STARTS at z = 36.703 -- inside that truncation --
           and overlaps the shank in x as well, over [-35.200, -5.754].

           THE CHECK THAT SETTLES IT is not the bounds but the assembled gap.
           Nearest-vertex distance between the three parts once all three
           transforms are applied, measured in world:

               wrist_roll <-> handle    0.3 mm
               wrist_roll <-> trigger   2.1 mm
               handle     <-> trigger   3.4 mm

           Sub-millimetre between the two parts that bolt together, and a few
           millimetres of running clearance to the part that pivots. That is a
           mechanical assembly, not three shapes that happen to be near each
           other -- tests/test_ghost.py asserts it, so a Menagerie bump or an STL
           refresh that broke the datum would fail rather than drift.

           TRIGGER, kept because the derivation is only as good as the datum
           claim and NOTHING HERE HAS BEEN SEEN ON A HEADSET: the first session
           that reports the handle is not where the hand is -> these seven
           numbers, and nothing else in this file or in teleop.py. The controller
           pose is written to the BODY, so the whole ghost is rigidly attached to
           the hand and this transform is exactly "where the hand grips the
           handle". -->
      <geom name="leader_ghost_handle" type="mesh" mesh="leader_handle"
            material="leader_ghost" pos="0 -0.000218 0.00095" quat="0 1 0 0"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0" mass="0" group="2"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
